RELATIONAL TRI-MODE UNIFIED SCHEMA FOR ALL QUADRICS
====================================================

A quadric surface Q is a relation expressed through three inseparable modes:
S-mode (Structure), I-mode (Interaction), D-mode (Dynamics).
Every quadric (non-degenerate or degenerate) is a specific S/I/D projection
of the same underlying second-degree relational form.

UNIVERSAL QUADRIC GENERATOR (RDG)
---------------------------------
Q = S[Q] + I[Q] + D[Q]

S[Q] = diag(A, B, C) acting on (x, y, z)
I[Q] = D*x*y + E*x*z + F*y*z
D[Q] = G*x + H*y + I*z + J

------------------------------------------------------------
TRI-MODE CORE
------------------------------------------------------------
S-mode:
  Structural invariants of the quadratic form.
  Signature of the squared terms (+, -, 0).
  Determines constraint geometry and admissible partitions.

I-mode:
  Couplings via mixed terms (xy, xz, yz) or opposing curvature signs.
  Valence propagation and relational curvature between axes.
  In canonical forms mixed terms are usually eliminated by rotation;
  residual I-mode appears as opposite-sign quadratic terms.

D-mode:
  Drift, unfolding, and directional behavior determined by the
  constant term after reduction to canonical form (1, -1, or 0).
  Precisely controls:
    - connectedness (one component vs two)
    - presence or absence of an apex
    - boundedness vs unbounded opening
    - number of sheets
    - extrusion direction when one variable is absent

------------------------------------------------------------
UNIFIED FORM
------------------------------------------------------------
Q(x, y, z) = A*x^2 + B*y^2 + C*z^2
           + D*x*y + E*x*z + F*y*z
           + G*x + H*y + I*z + J

SID = (signs of A, B, C | sign of constant after reduction)
PED = (canonical equation + geometric cue)

------------------------------------------------------------
THE NINE NON-DEGENERATE QUADRICS
------------------------------------------------------------

Ellipsoid
  SID: (+ + + | +)
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 + z^2/c^2 = 1
  RDG: Pure positive S-mode; closed and bounded D-mode (compact surface).

Elliptic Cylinder
  SID: (+ + 0 | +)
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 = 1
  RDG: S-mode missing one axis; D-mode extrudes the ellipse along the free axis.

Hyperboloid of One Sheet
  SID: (+ + - | +)
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 - z^2/c^2 = 1
  RDG: Mixed S-mode; D-mode = +1 keeps the surface connected (one sheet).

Hyperbolic Paraboloid (Saddle)
  SID: (+ - 0 | 0)
  PED: z = x^2/a^2 - y^2/b^2
  I-mode: Opposite-sign quadratic terms produce negative Gaussian curvature
          and saddle interaction between the two axes (curvature inversion).
  RDG: Residual I-mode via opposing signs; D-mode = 0 yields open unbounded saddle.

Elliptic Paraboloid
  SID: (+ + 0 | 0)
  PED: z = x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2
  RDG: Same-sign S-mode; D-mode = 0 opens in one direction only.

Hyperboloid of Two Sheets
  SID: (+ + - | -)
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 - z^2/c^2 = -1
  RDG: Same S-mode as one-sheet; D-mode = -1 splits the surface into two disconnected sheets.

Elliptic Cone (Double Cone)
  SID: (+ + - | 0)
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 - z^2/c^2 = 0
  RDG: Balanced mixed S-mode; D-mode = 0 produces an apex at the origin and two nappes.

Hyperbolic Cylinder
  SID: (+ - 0 | +)
  PED: x^2/a^2 - y^2/b^2 = 1
  RDG: Hyperbolic S-mode; D-mode extrudes the hyperbola along the free axis.

Parabolic Cylinder
  SID: (+ 0 0 | 0)
  PED: y = a*x^2
  RDG: Single-axis S-mode; D-mode extrudes the parabola along the free axis.

------------------------------------------------------------
DEGENERATE QUADRICS
------------------------------------------------------------
Degenerate cases arise when the quadratic form has reduced rank
or the full 4×4 matrix drops rank, collapsing dimension or producing
linear factors.

Point
  SID: (+ + + | 0)
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 + z^2/c^2 = 0
  RDG: Positive S-mode collapses under D-mode = 0 to a single point (origin).

Pair of Intersecting Planes
  SID: (+ - 0 | 0) or similar reduced forms
  PED: x^2 - y^2 = 0   (or x*y = 0 after rotation)
  RDG: S-mode factors into linear terms; D-mode = 0 yields two planes through the origin.

Pair of Parallel Planes
  SID: (+ 0 0 | -) or (+ 0 0 | +)
  PED: x^2 = 1   or   x^2 = -1 (empty) or x^2 = 0 (double plane)
  RDG: Single-axis S-mode; D-mode produces two parallel planes, one double plane, or empty set.

Single Plane
  SID: (0 0 0 | linear terms only)
  PED: ax + by + cz + d = 0
  RDG: Pure linear (degenerate S-mode); no quadratic structure remains.

Line (or Pair of Imaginary Planes intersecting in a real line)
  SID: reduced rank with mixed signs
  PED: x^2 + y^2 = 0
  RDG: Positive S-mode in two variables collapses under D-mode = 0 to the z-axis.

Empty Set
  SID: (+ + + | -) or (+ 0 0 | -)
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 + z^2/c^2 = -1
  RDG: Positive S-mode with incompatible D-mode sign yields no real points.

Double Plane
  SID: (+ 0 0 | 0)
  PED: x^2 = 0
  RDG: Single-axis S-mode with D-mode = 0 collapses to a plane of multiplicity two.

------------------------------------------------------------
UNIFIED RELATIONAL DYNAMIC GEOMETRY
------------------------------------------------------------
All quadrics (degenerate and non-degenerate) differ only by:
  - S-mode signature (signs and rank of squared terms)
  - I-mode couplings (mixed terms or residual opposing-sign curvature)
  - D-mode constant sign (1, -1, or 0) after reduction

The tri-mode schema generates every quadric from one relational second-degree form.

====================================================
APPENDIX: UNIFIED QUADRICS SCHEMA 
====================================================
Invariant Formulation

Every real affine quadric is determined, up to Euclidean congruence and scale,
by the inertia and rank of its quadratic part, the rank of its homogeneous
4x4 matrix, and the translated constant/linear residual.

A real affine quadric is determined, up to Euclidean congruence and scale,
by the inertia and rank of its quadratic part, the rank of its homogeneous
4x4 matrix, and the translated constant/linear residual. These data are
interpreted relationally as:

  S-mode : quadratic inertia and rank of the symmetric matrix M
  I-mode : principal-axis (eigenvector) coupling structure of M
  D-mode : affine unfolding data after translation (constant or linear residual)

The one-sheet and two-sheet hyperboloids share the same S-mode inertia
(2,1,0) and differ only in the sign of the D-mode residual constant,
which decides connectedness.

------------------------------------------------------------
TRI-MODE CORE
------------------------------------------------------------
Invariant

S-mode:
  Inertia type (n+, n-, n0) of the symmetric matrix
    M = [[A, D/2, E/2],
         [D/2, B, F/2],
         [E/2, F/2, C]]
  together with rank(M). This is coordinate-invariant.

I-mode:
  Eigenvector frame of M (principal axes).
  Mixed terms xy, xz, yz are a coordinate-dependent shadow of this
  coupling structure and can be removed by orthogonal rotation.

D-mode:
  After translation (completing the square), the residual class:
    - nonzero constant (= ±1 after scaling)
    - zero constant
    - essential linear term in one variable
  Controls connectedness, number of sheets, apex, and extrusion.

Full invariant data also includes rank of the 4x4 homogeneous matrix
    ~Q = [[A, D/2, E/2, G/2],
          [D/2, B, F/2, H/2],
          [E/2, F/2, C, I/2],
          [G/2, H/2, I/2, J]]

------------------------------------------------------------
UNIFIED FORM
------------------------------------------------------------
Q(x) = x^T M x + b^T x + c = 0
where b = (G, H, I)^T

Classification key:
  QuadricType = (inertia(M), rank(M), rank(~Q), translated residual class)

------------------------------------------------------------
THE FIVE NONDEGENERATE REAL QUADRICS
------------------------------------------------------------

Ellipsoid
  Inertia: (3,0,0)    Residual: nonzero constant
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 + z^2/c^2 = 1
  RDG: Positive-definite S-mode; closed bounded D-mode.

Hyperboloid of One Sheet
  Inertia: (2,1,0)    Residual: nonzero constant (matching sign)
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 - z^2/c^2 = 1
  RDG: Mixed S-mode; D-mode keeps the level set connected (one sheet).

Hyperboloid of Two Sheets
  Inertia: (2,1,0)    Residual: opposite nonzero constant
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 - z^2/c^2 = -1
  RDG: Same S-mode as one-sheet; opposite D-mode residual splits into two sheets.

Elliptic Paraboloid
  Inertia: (2,0,0)    Residual: essential linear term
  PED: z = x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2
  RDG: Positive semi-definite S-mode; D-mode opens in one direction.

Hyperbolic Paraboloid (Saddle)
  Inertia: (1,1,0)    Residual: essential linear term
  PED: z = x^2/a^2 - y^2/b^2
  I-mode: Opposing eigenvalues produce negative Gaussian curvature
          (saddle interaction between principal axes).
  RDG: Indefinite S-mode of rank 2; open unbounded D-mode.

------------------------------------------------------------
ADDITIONAL TRUE QUADRIC SURFACES
------------------------------------------------------------
Often classified as degenerate in projective geometry

Elliptic Cone
  Inertia: (2,1,0)    Residual: zero constant after centering
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 - z^2/c^2 = 0
  RDG: Mixed S-mode; D-mode residual = 0 produces apex and two nappes.

Elliptic Cylinder
  Inertia: (2,0,0)    Residual: one zero eigen-direction
  PED: x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 = 1
  RDG: S-mode of rank 2; D-mode extrudes along the null direction.

Hyperbolic Cylinder
  Inertia: (1,1,0)    Residual: one zero eigen-direction
  PED: x^2/a^2 - y^2/b^2 = 1
  RDG: Indefinite S-mode of rank 2; D-mode extrudes the hyperbola.

Parabolic Cylinder
  Inertia: (1,0,0)    Residual: zero eigen-direction + linear term
  PED: y = a*x^2
  RDG: S-mode of rank 1; D-mode extrudes the parabola.

------------------------------------------------------------
DEGENERATE CASES (Selected)
------------------------------------------------------------

Point
  Inertia: (3,0,0)    Residual: zero
  PED: x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 0

Pair of Intersecting Planes
  Inertia: (1,1,0) or reduced
  PED: x^2 - y^2 = 0

Pair of Parallel Planes
  Inertia: (1,0,0)    Residual: nonzero constant
  PED: x^2 = 1

Double Plane
  Inertia: (1,0,0)    Residual: zero
  PED: x^2 = 0

Empty Set (Imaginary Ellipsoid)
  Inertia: (3,0,0)    Residual: incompatible constant
  PED: x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = -1
